Z||000000||Hokie||12-16-1999||12:37 PM||||The Fiero is an 86 SE automatic with V6, 60000 miles.  The other night the car started fine, but stalled as soon as I put in into drive or reverse.  It did this several times and always started up okay and ran fine in neutral and park.  After several attempts, it quit stalling and ran fine.  I also noticed that the gas guage was reading about an eighth of a tank, so I immediately drove to a gas station and put gas in it.  Since then, I haven't driven it much but it hasn't stalled anymore. In a possibly related issue, when the car is cold, the volts light comes on when engine is first started and stays on until the engine is revved once.  I don't think it is a guage problem because I can tell that the blinkers are slower.  Also, when the blinkers are on, the volts needle drops in rythm with them.  Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||dogtired||12-16-1999||02:32 PM||||Couple of thoughts here?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>My daughter who continually runs out of gas, is slowly learning that 1/4 is empty. I would think after she had to walk three times, that would be enough?? Anyway, due to the quality of the fine guages in our little gems, there is a good chance your fuel pump wasn't picking up gas at 1/8 tank?<P>Now on to the Volts? Could be your belts are a bit loose? (that is what I would check first) On the other hand, it could be your cold start injector (right behind the distributor) isn't injecting, which would give you a bit slower RPM on initial start?<BR>Just my seat of the pants reaction?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||12-16-1999||03:06 PM||||Clean all your grounds! My 84's voltmeter waved like that with the blinker on, and It got alot better after I scrubbed all the grounds.  Your alternator could be headin' south on you, though.  I know mines not perfect and the needle still swings a little bit.||||reg||1
